<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napoleon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Napoleon Bonaparte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Napoleon Bonaparte was a French military leader who seized power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and ruled as Emperor of the French. His conquests reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: he dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, and his occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> helped trigger the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. His invasion of Portugal drove the Portuguese court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1808, a step towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" width="252" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Napoleon Bonaparte was born Napoleone di Buonaparte on 15 August 1769 in Ajaccio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, a year after the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> ceded the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His family was of Italian origin, the Buonapartes descending from a minor Tuscan noble family that had moved to Corsica in the 16th century. With the support of the French governor Charles Louis de Marbeuf, his father's patron, he obtained a royal bursary to a military academy on the mainland, and in September 1785 he was commissioned a second lieutenant in the artillery. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, but after a failed attempt to take Ajaccio in June 1793 the family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Napoleon rose rapidly after winning the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> in 1793, and in 1796 a campaign against the Austrians and their Italian allies made him a national hero. In 1798 he led an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. On 9 November 1799 (18 Brumaire) he and his fellow conspirators launched a coup against the Directory, and he became First Consul. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Battle of Marengo</a> in 1800, sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1803 and crowned himself Emperor of the French in December 1804.</p><p>He defeated the Third Coalition at Austerlitz, which led to the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and beat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1806 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1807. In 1808 he installed his brother Joseph as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, provoking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>. His invasion of Russia in 1812 ended in a catastrophic winter retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. After defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813 he abdicated in April 1814 and was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>. He escaped ten months later, but the Seventh Coalition defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> in June 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The British sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a> in the South Atlantic, where he died on 5 May 1821 of stomach cancer, aged 51. His reforms included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, a system of public education, the Banque de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Concordat of 1801 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, and he gave civil and religious equality to Protestants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Against this stand wars that historians say cost six million lives, the plunder of conquered territories, the abolition of the free press and of directly elected representative government, and the reinstatement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in French colonies. Historians still debate whether he was an enlightened despot who laid the foundations of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> or a man who brought great misery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Napoleon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the French Revolution the Republic refused in 1793 to accept the quèstia, rejecting the French co-sovereignty as a feudal relic, and for over a decade the bishop was the only co-prince until <b>Napoleon</b> restored the French rights in 1806 at the Andorrans' request.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Napoleon</b> had made himself Emperor of the French in May, and the elective Holy Roman crown was visibly failing after the territorial upheavals of the Imperial Recess of 1803, so Francis took a new hereditary title, Emperor of Austria, as Francis I. The title gave a common name…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Coastal towers and Fort Regent were built against <b>Napoleon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Napoleon</b>’s Act of Mediation of 1803 restored Zürich as a canton of the Swiss Confederation, now with rural representation, and the liberal movement launched at the Ustertag of 1830 brought equal rights for town and country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1807 <b>Napoleon</b> created the Duchy of Warsaw as a French client state from land taken from Prussia, and enlarged it at Austria's expense after the Battle of Wagram in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1807, fearing that <b>Napoleon</b> would seize the Danish navy, Britain bombarded Copenhagen and carried off the fleet, driving Denmark-Norway into alliance with France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ajaccio, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Longwood House. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.93,8.74&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Ajaccio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4762 671 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Napoleon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.93,8.74&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/josephine-de-beauharnais"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/josephine-de-beauharnais.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Joséphine de Beauharnais</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1763–1814</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/marie-louise-duchess-of-parma"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/marie-louise-duchess-of-parma.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Marie Louise, Duchess of Parma</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1791–1847</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
