<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is my wish that my ashes may repose on the banks of the Seine, in the midst of the French people, whom I have loved so well.&#8221;</p><footer>His will, 1821 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon%27s_Addresses/Napoleon%27s_Will" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Soldiers of my old guard, I bid you farewell. For twenty years I have constantly accompanied you on the road to honor and glory.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell to the Old Guard, Fontainebleau, 1814 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon%27s_Addresses/Part_V" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My waking thoughts are all of thee. Your portrait and the remembrance of last night's delirium have robbed my senses of repose.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Josephine, 1796 &#183; tr. Henry Foljambe Hall, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37499/pg37499-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is on the field of battle, amid the sufferings of a multitude of wounded, and surrounded by fifteen thousand corpses, that I beseech your Majesty to listen to the voice of humanity…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Emperor of Austria from the field of Marengo, June 1800 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon's_Addresses/Part_III" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Among the Russians there are no generals in contending against whom I can acquire any glory. All I wish is to obtain the victory with the least possible bloodshed. My soldiers are my children.&#8221;</p><footer>Proclamation to the army after Ulm, October 1805 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon's_Addresses/Part_IV" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But what a romance is my life!&#8221;</p><footer>Conversation at St Helena (Las Cases, Memorial of St Helena, 1823) &#183; English translation, London 1836 (translator not named) &#183; as recorded by his companion in exile, the Count de Las Cases &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53968/pg53968-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
